{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "The next morning Mira woke to gulls and the soft knock of hulls against the pilings. Tobias was already gone, his boots missing from beside the door, the kettle still faintly warm. She found him where she expected, at the top of the old lighthouse, polishing a lens that no longer needed polishing. \"Habit,\" he said, before she could tease him. From up there the whole village lay open like a hand: the church with its crooked weathervane, the schoolhouse, the green where the fair used to be. Smoke curled from chimneys that had been cold for years. Someone was hanging washing on Gull Row, and the sheets snapped white and bright. \"That's the Abernethy girl,\" Tobias said, squinting. \"The one who drowned in the storm of '52.\" Mira looked, and the girl was a grown woman now, singing as she pinned. Down at the quay the young man from yesterday was helping mend nets, sleeves rolled, laughing at something the harbormaster said. He looked up once toward the lighthouse and raised a hand. Tobias raised his in return. \"He asked me how long he'd be staying,\" Tobias said. \"What did you tell him?\" \"That nobody's in a hurry here.\" Mira leaned against the rail. The sea stretched out silver and endless, and far on the horizon another sail was coming, small as a moth. Tobias set down his cloth. \"I'll just go and see,\" he said, and she let him, because she knew he would always go, and because he would always come back."}
